// test_bridge_v1_lowwater_bigjam_gas.prm
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// BIG-JAM VARIANT of test_bridge_v1_lowwater_logjam_floaterlog_gas.prm (the confirmed-working
// low-water log-jam scenario, job 4134882 -- 0 spurious tunnel contacts, natural buoyant
// spread, several logs cleanly through the bridge). That run's 9 logs never actually produced
// a sustained clog -- individual logs snagged briefly and released. This variant deliberately
// pushes toward real clogging:
//
//   - LOG COUNT: 9 -> 54 -> 108 (doubled again). Same 9 upstream aim points (X=15/22, same Y
//     targets) as before, but each position now gets 12 stacked logs instead of 1, released in
//     waves 3000 steps apart (tighter than the ~4000-4500 step transit time from spawn to bridge
//     established earlier this session -- so by design each new wave's log should arrive while
//     the PREVIOUS wave's log is still at or near the opening, creating actual queueing pressure
//     instead of one log passing before the next even leaves upstream). Wave 0 offsets match the
//     original 9-log schedule (5000-15000); waves 1-11 add +3000 each. Max settlingSteps 48000,
//     leaving 52000 steps of tail time to watch the jam (or lack of one) persist/evolve. Waves
//     7-12 reuse the same Z=55-65 slots as waves 1-6 (see next bullet) -- by wave 7's activation
//     (18000 steps after wave 1's), wave 1 has long since drifted to its buoyant equilibrium
//     (~51-52) and moved off downstream, so re-using the Z slot introduces no new spawn-overlap
//     risk beyond what's already validated below for the original 6-wave design.
//   - SPAWN HEIGHT: Z-staggering (55-65) COLLAPSED back to a single shared Z=70 per aim point,
//     raised well above the old band. Two independent problems the Z-stagger scheme never
//     addressed, found by inspecting job 4139833's clean 100000-step run:
//       (a) elongated floaters tumbling on arrival at the bridge could swing a far corner deep
//           into an arch wall in a single sub-step (see numSubCycles comment below) -- unrelated
//           to spawn height, fixed via more sub-cycles instead.
//       (b) fast early-spawned floaters complete a FULL LAP of the periodic (X) domain in as few
//           as ~22000 steps -- well before the release schedule finishes at step 48000 -- and
//           re-enter near X=0-5, right past the X=15/22 spawn region, while later waves are still
//           dormant or just activating. Raising the spawn height alone doesn't stop this (a
//           looped floater is back at ordinary equilibrium height by the time it returns), but a
//           single, deliberately-higher shared Z gives more headroom/predictability than the old
//           6-value stagger for the genuinely-fixed problem below (same-spot double-occupancy at
//           activation), while removing complexity that wasn't buying anything against (a) or (b).
//     Original Z-stagger rationale, kept for the record: staggering Z instead of
//     stacking at the exact same point. FIRST ATTEMPT at this used the exact same (X,Y,Z) for all
//     waves,
//     relying purely on startInactive's fluid/DEM exclusion (SetupStages.h::
//     mapParticlesOntoCells, BuildTimeloop.h::addSweepFluidParticleCoupling,
//     ParticleKernels.h::doDEMSubCycles) to keep dormant copies safely invisible -- that part
//     is correct, but it missed that the exclusion only protects DORMANT copies from each
//     other; it does nothing once a new wave ACTIVATES, and the newly-activated log's spawn
//     point can still be occupied by its own predecessor if that predecessor hasn't drifted
//     away yet. Confirmed via a real run (job 4135900): the wave-0 log at (15,55) had only
//     moved ~3.4 LU in 3000 steps (acceleration from rest takes real time -- nowhere near the
//     ~30 LU a steady v=0.01 drift would predict), so wave-1 activated directly on top of it,
//     producing a 1.27+ LU instantaneous interpenetration and a violent corrective launch (one
//     resulting trajectory reached Z=87.7, within 2.3 LU of the domain ceiling; another
//     actually contacted the Z=90 top boundary directly). This is a genuine double-occupancy
//     bug, not a normal (and entirely intended) log-vs-log collision once they're actually
//     both drifting free -- the fix only needs to guarantee a new wave's SPAWN point can never
//     overlap wherever its predecessor currently is, not prevent collisions altogether.
//     Z-staggering the spawn point does this unconditionally, regardless of how slowly a given
//     wave happens to drift: by the time wave N activates, wave N-1 has already settled to its
//     natural buoyant equilibrium height (~51-52 here) whether or not it has moved far
//     horizontally, so spawning wave N a few LU higher (safely more than the log's own 3 LU
//     height) than the water-level equilibrium keeps them from ever occupying the same volume
//     at the activation instant. Buoyancy pulls each log down to the same equilibrium height
//     shortly after release regardless of its starting Z, so this doesn't change where/how they
//     end up floating -- only how they're introduced.
//   - TIMESTEPS: 30000 -> 100000, to give the 54-log release sequence (finishing at step
//     30000) a long tail to actually develop/observe a persistent jam rather than cutting off
//     right as the last wave arrives.
//   - FLOATER LOGGING: was OFF (logFloaterHistory true -> false) for the original 100000-step
//     bigjam runs -- the per-subcycle CSV log was for diagnosing the tunnel-contact bugs, and
//     with 54 floaters x 100000 steps it would be enormous. RE-ENABLED (false -> true) for this
//     run specifically to investigate job 4140712's pier-envelope crash (see ModelParameters'
//     own comment on this flag) -- expect a large rank_<N>.csv under floater-history/.
//   - DOMAIN HEIGHT: domainSize.z 180 -> 90 (cellsPerBlock.z 30 -> 15, keeping the SAME 72
//     total blocks/ranks: 128/32 * 60/20 * 90/15 = 4*3*6 = 72, so the existing --ntasks=72 job
//     script needs no change). Deck top is Z=76 -- the old 180 gave ~104 LU of pure headroom
//     above it, never used by anything in this low-water scenario. 90 gives ~14 LU above the
//     deck top instead: enough for a real splash/momentary pile-up without paying to simulate
//     100+ LU of empty gas every step, and this run's own domain-Z reduction alone roughly
//     halves the per-step fluid cell count, offsetting a good chunk of the 3.3x step-count
//     increase from the timesteps change above.
//
// Everything else (piers, arches, ArchTunnels, targetMeanVelocityMagnitude=0.01, low-water
// liquidHeightFactor=2.0, Re/Fr/We, model parameters) is verbatim from
// test_bridge_v1_lowwater_logjam_floaterlog_gas.prm -- see that file's own header, and
// test_bridge_v1_lowwater_gas.prm before it, for the full derivation.
//
// COLLISION MATERIAL: restitutionCoefficient 0.5 -> 0.3. These floaters are logs, and logs in a
// river/bridge log-jam scenario are floating debris that's been soaking in water, not dry stored
// timber -- wet/waterlogged wood-wood impacts dissipate substantially more energy than dry
// wood-wood, so 0.3 is the more physically appropriate coefficient of restitution for this
// material. frictionCoefficient left at 0.5 (unchanged, not part of this request).
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

BlockForestParameters
{
   cellsPerBlock                 < 32, 20, 15 >;
   periodicity                   < 1, 0, 0 >;
   loadSnapshot                  false;
   storeSnapshot                 true;
   snapshotFrequency             10000;
   snapshotBaseFolder            snapshots;
}

DomainParameters
{
   domainSize         <128, 60, 90>;
   wavePeriods        1;
   liquidHeightFactor 2.0;  // low-water, unchanged -- see lowwater base's header; away-baseline absoluteLiquidHeight~49.1
   floorHeightFactor  4.1393;
   initialAmplitude   0;
}

PIDParameters
{
   targetMeanVelocityMagnitude 0.01;
   proportionalGain            2e-4;
   derivativeGain              1e-6;
   integralGain                2e-4;
   maxRamp                     1e-4;
   minActuatingVariable        0;
   maxActuatingVariable        1e-3;
}

PhysicsParameters
{
   enableWetting     false;
   timesteps         100000;
   Re                3100;
   Fr                1.31;
   We                15.6188;
   useHardcodedUnits true;
   // no gravityOverride -- dynamically computed gravity
}

ParticleParameters
{
   inFileName spheres_out.dat;
   bedCopiesInX 1;
   bedCopiesInY 1;
   densityRatio 2.55;
   fixingHeightFactor 1.5;
   frictionCoefficient 0.5;
   restitutionCoefficient 0.3; // wet/waterlogged wood-wood -- these are floating logs, soaked, not dry timber
   numSubCycles 40; // 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> 40. 20 got job 4139553 all the way to a clean 200000-step
                     // finish (with useAnalyticalContactDetection also on) -- no repeat of the
                     // NaN-quaternion crash from job 4139454. 30 was for the SEPARATE "flying
                     // cuboid" events found by inspecting that clean run: elongated (12 LU) box
                     // floaters tumbling on arrival at the bridge entrance can swing a far corner
                     // several LU into an arch tunnel wall within a single sub-step even though
                     // their centre is nowhere close. This further bump (job 4140767 postmortem)
                     // is for a THIRD, distinct mechanism: two floating boxes colliding with each
                     // other right at pier 1's location while one of them simultaneously envelops
                     // the pillar's thin cylinder -- confirmed via the floater-history CSV
                     // (detectionPath="pillar exterior (Box, ENVELOP-clamped)") that the SAME
                     // clamped overlap recurred unchanged across 5 consecutive sub-cycles instead
                     // of separating, compounding into a ~500-magnitude force. Finer sub-cycling
                     // means the box travels less between force recomputations, giving the
                     // (now also tightened, see ParticleKernels.h maxOverlap 1.5->1.0) pillar
                     // spring more chances to actually separate the geometry instead of re-firing
                     // at the same clamped depth.
   useLubricationCorrection true;
   useNoSlipParticles false;
   useParticleBed false;
}

// DEM bodies matching the BodyBC piers exactly (BodyBC is LBM-only -- floaters need a real mesa_pd
// body to collide with, and nothing else creates one). 4 cylinders (the piers' round end-caps,
// radius 5) + 2 boxes (the connecting box between them, halfExtents = half of X=61-67/Y=10-20-or-
// 40-50/Z=0-76). center Z is cosmetic (CylindricalBoundary is infinite along its axis).
CylinderPillars
{
   CylinderPillar { center < 61, 15, 38 >; radius 5; }
   CylinderPillar { center < 67, 15, 38 >; radius 5; }
   CylinderPillar { center < 61, 45, 38 >; radius 5; }
   CylinderPillar { center < 67, 45, 38 >; radius 5; }
}

Pillars
{
   Pillar { center < 64, 15, 38 >; halfExtents < 3, 5, 38 >; }
   Pillar { center < 64, 45, 38 >; halfExtents < 3, 5, 38 >; }
}

// One entry per arch, matching the BodyBC arch cylinders below exactly (center Y/Z, radius).
// xMin/xMax DELIBERATELY set to the piers' own straight-box span (61..67). Not mesa_pd bodies --
// consumed directly by ParticleKernels.h's ComputeTunnelContacts, which only engages the UPPER
// semicircle (Z >= center[2] = the springing line, 56) -- confirmed clean (0 spurious contacts,
// 100% of real contacts above the springing line) on both the lowwater run (job 4134882) and a
// dedicated raised-water run built specifically to force genuine arch interaction (job 4135755).
// deckTopZ 76 added (job investigation into "does the code model the arch's front/back face
// and top?"): each ArchTunnel now also creates a real deck-fill solid box from its own crown
// (center.z+radius) up to the deck top (matching the BodyBC arch boxes' own max Z, 76 below) --
// see ArchTunnelParams' own comment (SimulationParams.h) and SetupStages.h's arch-body creation
// loop for the full derivation. yMin/yMax deliberately left at their defaults (center.y -+
// radius): this bridge's own BodyBC arch boxes have Y-span EXACTLY equal to each arch's own
// diameter (confirmed directly: left/right 10 = 2*5, central 20 = 2*10), so there is no separate
// flat haunch wall to add beside the curve -- the curve itself is the front/back face below the
// crown, already handled by the existing curved containment body.
ArchTunnels
{
   ArchTunnel { center < 0, 5, 56 >;  radius 5;  xMin 61; xMax 67; deckTopZ 76; }  // left arch
   ArchTunnel { center < 0, 30, 56 >; radius 10; xMin 61; xMax 67; deckTopZ 76; }  // central arch
   ArchTunnel { center < 0, 55, 56 >; radius 5;  xMin 61; xMax 67; deckTopZ 76; }  // right arch
}

// Log-jam debris: 9 upstream aim points (2 rows: X=15/X=22), 6 stacked logs per point (54
// total), staggered release (startInactive + settlingSteps) in 3000-step waves -- see this
// file's own header for the full rationale (why 6x the log count, why waves are spaced tighter
// than the transit time, why overlapping spawn positions are safe).
FloatingBoxes
{
   // (15,5) -> left arch: 12 spawn-separated (Z-staggered) logs, 3000-step waves (doubled from 6;
   // waves 7-12 reuse the same Z=55-65 slots -- by wave 7's activation (18000 steps after wave 1's),
   // wave 1 has long since drifted to its buoyant equilibrium and away downstream, so re-using the
   // Z slot is safe per this file's own overlap analysis above)
   FloatingBox { position < 15, 5, 70 >; halfExtents < 6, 1, 1.5 >; densityRatio 0.6; settlingSteps   5000; startInactive true; }
   FloatingBox { position < 15, 5, 70 >; halfExtents < 6, 1, 1.5 >; densityRatio 0.6; settlingSteps   8000; startInactive true; }
   FloatingBox { position < 15, 5, 70 >; halfExtents < 6, 1, 1.5 >; densityRatio 0.6; settlingSteps  11000; startInactive true; }
